  "articleBody": "If you are planning a bathroom renovation, hiring the right tile installer makes the difference between an enduring, waterproof retreat and a costly leak. Tile and stone are beautiful cosmetic finishes, but they are not inherently waterproof on their own. Water easily penetrates standard grout joints, which means the unseen assembly behind the tile does the critical work of protecting your home's framing.\n\nBefore you accept a quote, ask specific questions about the installer's technical approach. A reliable contractor builds on a rigid, clean substrate and installs a bonded waterproof membrane meeting ANSI A118.10 standards in wet areas. Make sure your installer understands TCNA Handbook guidelines for substrate flatness, especially if you have picked trendy large-format tiles. Ask whether they plan to use flexible sealant instead of rigid grout at plane changes, like inside corners and tub joints, where house settling naturally triggers cracking.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start:\nHave your selected tile specifications ready, including thickness, slip-resistance rating, and whether it requires specialized thin-set mortar or penetrating sealer. Confirm how the crew plans to test shower pans for leaks prior to tile setting. When you need structural subfloor leveling, custom shower pans, or marble installation, a dedicated general and finish contractor is essential. Call Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to discuss your tile project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
